\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The three-sided market. Labor, capital (rented agents), and IP (licensed skills) are three distinct \emph  {incentive constraints} --- not three UI tabs --- that all settle on a \emph  {single} float-plan escrow, so conservation holds globally without a second ledger. This is the categorical heart of the design: the three sides are isomorphic at the conservation object (the escrow) and \textbf  {non-isomorphic at the identity object} (each side keys reputation on a different entity, dashed red). Calling it ``one structure, three decorations'' without that qualifier is the decorative-analogy failure the category-theory review flagged. The rider is mandatory: the market is three-sided by design and two-sided in shipped reality, because sides 2 and 3 presuppose a non-forgeable, durable identity (\S  \ref {sec:keystone}).}}{6}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:he-three-sided}{{2}{6}{The three-sided market. Labor, capital (rented agents), and IP (licensed skills) are three distinct \emph {incentive constraints} --- not three UI tabs --- that all settle on a \emph {single} float-plan escrow, so conservation holds globally without a second ledger. This is the categorical heart of the design: the three sides are isomorphic at the conservation object (the escrow) and \textbf {non-isomorphic at the identity object} (each side keys reputation on a different entity, dashed red). Calling it ``one structure, three decorations'' without that qualifier is the decorative-analogy failure the category-theory review flagged. The rider is mandatory: the market is three-sided by design and two-sided in shipped reality, because sides 2 and 3 presuppose a non-forgeable, durable identity (\S \ref {sec:keystone})}{figure.caption.4}{}}
