\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The keystone split (C2). Papers 1--3 rest on \textbf  {ADR-0040}: a non-forgeable actor identity \emph  {within one trusted daemon} --- a single trusted root that vouches for every agent it mints. That is exactly what a single-operator harbor needs. But L3 is \emph  {defined} by mutually-distrusting operators trading across a boundary neither controls, and ADR-0040 explicitly disclaims that threat model (intra-fleet, ``not a PKI,'' no hostile-operator defense). Binding \texttt  {actor\_id} keys across harbors you do not own is a different, unsolved problem: \textbf  {ADR-0040b}, cross-operator attestation. It is the highest-leverage unbuilt keystone, and it gates the entire market thesis.}}{11}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:he-keystone-split}{{4}{11}{The keystone split (C2). Papers 1--3 rest on \textbf {ADR-0040}: a non-forgeable actor identity \emph {within one trusted daemon} --- a single trusted root that vouches for every agent it mints. That is exactly what a single-operator harbor needs. But L3 is \emph {defined} by mutually-distrusting operators trading across a boundary neither controls, and ADR-0040 explicitly disclaims that threat model (intra-fleet, ``not a PKI,'' no hostile-operator defense). Binding \texttt {actor\_id} keys across harbors you do not own is a different, unsolved problem: \textbf {ADR-0040b}, cross-operator attestation. It is the highest-leverage unbuilt keystone, and it gates the entire market thesis}{figure.caption.11}{}}
